    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i think i'm getting the vibe of that. so its almost like you're checking for overlap between the signal and these pure waves. is there any way the math can be fooled by stuff that looks like a frequency but isn't actually part of the signal? like noise or something?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've grasped the core concept: the Fourier Transform measures the \"overlap\" or correlation between your signal and pure sine waves. However, mathematically speaking, pure sine waves are \"eigenfunctions\" of linear systems, meaning no matter what noise or distortion you add, the decomposition remains accurate and unique because these basis functions span the entire space of possible signals. While real-world sampling can introduce artifacts if your data is too sparse (the aliasing problem), the theory itself ensures that every measurable pattern—whether clean or noisy—is mathematically forced into its specific frequency components precisely.",
      "source": "target",
